---
title: Elastic / On-Demand Architecture
authors: [Lewis Lan]
broadcast: 5
order: 2
---

New York’s cultural institutions are often understood through their buildings. The Met, MoMA, and the Whitney accumulate galleries, auditoriums, archives, offices, restaurants, and event spaces within increasingly large architectural envelopes. For smaller nonprofits, however, the relationship between institution and building can operate very differently.

Carriage Trade, Blank Forms, and Storefront for Art and Architecture suggest three different modes of flexibility within similarly modest physical footprints. Carriage Trade operates with an unusually lean organizational structure, using a compact gallery not to represent institutional scale but to construct relationships between artists, histories, and contemporary political conditions. Blank Forms similarly maintains a small-scale Brooklyn base while supporting exhibitions, performances, publications, archives, and artist estates. Its programming regularly exceeds its physical footprint, appearing at spaces including Dia Chelsea, Greene Naftali, New York Studio School, and McNally Jackson Gallery.

At Storefront for Art and Architecture, this flexibility becomes architectural. Steven Holl and Vito Acconci’s 12 hinged facade panels allow the boundary between gallery and street to continually change. Rather than adding another room, the existing envelope opens, rotates, and temporarily appropriates the sidewalk as part of the institution. A small fixed footprint therefore produces multiple spatial conditions.

