{% figura src="/images/vignettes/lewis-07.jpg" alt="Facade of Storefront for Art and Architecture with its hinged panels swung open onto the sidewalk" pie="1.5. Storefront for Art and Architecture" /%}

This elasticity can extend from space into the organizational infrastructure that supports it as well. CANNY (Collaborative Arts Network New York)connects fifteen New York based nonprofits through collective fundraising, organizational initiatives, and resource sharing. ArtsPool pushes this further by pooling back-office functions such as accounting, payroll, HR, and benefits across multiple organizations. If administration itself can become shared infrastructure, each institution no longer needs to reproduce every function within its own footprint.

The case studies therefore suggest three ways an institution can become elastic: transform what it has, temporarily occupy what surrounds it, and share what it does not need to own. Physical footprint is no longer necessarily a limitation. The city becomes an extension of the institution, allowing cultural organizations to grow and contract without carrying the permanent financial and spatial burden.
